From 2e50ad0eb18c30027d7588abd293f17e26891b46 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PyScotch <pyscotch@noreply.invalid>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 16:11:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Select the partitioning objective of `METIS_PartMeshDual()`
 from `options[METIS_OPTION_OBJTYPE]` only

`METIS_PartMeshDual()` returned the communication volume whenever a
`vsize` array was provided, whatever the requested objective. As for
the MeTiS v5 graph partitioning routines, the objective is now read
from the option array only, and checked: edge cut ignores the vertex
sizes, communication volume uses them, unit sizes being assumed when
none are provided.

The test program now checks the returned objective values against the
dual graph of the mesh, and the documentation describes the supported
options.

Refs: CLI-19, CLI-05
---
 doc/src/scotch/s_l.tex                     | 10 ++-
 src/check/test_libmetis_dual.c             | 94 ++++++++++++++++++++--
 src/libscotchmetis/metis_graph_part_dual.c | 19 +++--
 3 files changed, 109 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/doc/src/scotch/s_l.tex b/doc/src/scotch/s_l.tex
index e701df1..5108e05 100644
--- a/doc/src/scotch/s_l.tex
+++ b/doc/src/scotch/s_l.tex
@@ -8973,10 +8973,16 @@ corresponding elements in the mesh.
 
 Conforming to the \metis\ API, the base value of the numbering
 can be defined by setting \texttt{options[METIS\_\lbt OPTION\_\lbt
-NUMBERING]}. It is the only option supported by \scotch.
+NUMBERING]}, and the partitioning objective by setting
+\texttt{options[METIS\_\lbt OPTION\_\lbt OBJTYPE]}: either the edge
+cut of the dual graph, or the communication volume, computed from the
+element communication sizes provided in the \texttt{vsize} array, or
+unit sizes if it is not provided. These are the only options supported
+by \scotch.
 
 On return, the \texttt{epart} and \texttt{npart} arrays contain the
-element and node partitions, respectively.
+element and node partitions, respectively, and \texttt{*objval} holds
+the value of the requested objective.
 If the function fails, \texttt{*objval} is set to a negative value
 that corresponds to the relevant \texttt{METIS\_\lbt ERROR\_*} code.
 \end{itemize}
